3⃣ Components
Definition:
Components are independent, reusable pieces of UI.
Types:
● Functional components
● Class components
Example:
function Greeting() {
return <h1>Hello User</h1>;
}
Usage:
<Greeting />

Explanation:
Component acts like a function that returns UI.
4⃣ Props (Properties)
Definition:
Props are used to pass data from parent to child component.
Example:
function Welcome(props) {
return <h1>Hello {props.name}</h1>;
}
function App() {
return <Welcome name="Mowa" />;
}
Output:
Hello Mowa
Key Points:
● Read-only
● Used for communication between components

5⃣ State
Definition:
State is used to store dynamic data in a component.
Example:
import { useState } from "react";
function Counter() {
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
return (
<>
<p>{count}</p>
<button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increase</button>
</>
);
}
Explanation:
● count → current value
● setCount → updates value
● UI updates automatically
6⃣ Error Handling in React
Definition:
Error handling is used to catch and manage errors in components.

Example:
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
constructor(props) {
super(props);
this.state = { hasError: false };
}
static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
return { hasError: true };
}
render() {
if (this.state.hasError) {
return <h1>Something went wrong</h1>;
}
return this.props.children;
}
}
Usage:
<ErrorBoundary>
<MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
7⃣ React Router
Definition:
Used to navigate between pages without reloading.

React Router
Example:
import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from "react-router-dom";
function Home() {
return <h1>Home Page</h1>;
}
function About() {
return <h1>About Page</h1>;
}
function App() {
return (
<BrowserRouter>
<Routes>
<Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
<Route path="/about" element={<About />} />
</Routes>
</BrowserRouter>
);
}
8⃣ Hooks
Definition:
Hooks allow you to use state and lifecycle features in functional components.

Common Hooks:
● useState
● useEffect
useState Example:
const [name, setName] = useState("Mowa");
useEffect Example:
import { useEffect } from "react";
useEffect(() => {
console.log("Component Mounted");
}, []);
Explanation:
● Runs when component loads
● Used for API calls, side effects
